From: Stanislaw Pal <kuncy7@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] frontends/nss: rebuild a multicast flow of a tag_8021q bridge
 on a membership change

When the last member of a group on a port of a routed multicast flow's
bridge leaves, the bridge update decelerates the flow and leaves the
rest to the MFC callback: a proxy is expected to take the bridge off the
route. A static route does not, and neither does a proxy that keeps the
bridge after the last leave. The connection then keeps its destination
list, the next packet the host forwards accelerates it again with the
departed port in it, and the stream keeps going out of that port until
the source stops. Measured by Adriel Santos on a Xiaomi AX3000T v2
(IPQ5018, AN8855 with the an8855-8021q tagger, static MFC from
smcroute): the stream stayed on the port after the MDB entry was gone,
with every channel a zapping receiver had watched adding to it, where
the host path stops two seconds after the leave.

On a bridge with tag_8021q DSA ports the rule names the conduit and the
bridge's VID for every port, and the switch delivers the frames; there is
no per-port part of the rule to edit. So when a port joins or leaves such
a bridge, make the connection defunct instead. The next packet builds it
again from the MFC and the bridge's MDB, as it would for a new flow. A
pure bridged flow with no port left goes the same way.

Three more things on the way:

- In the create path, the ports of such a bridge all resolve to the same
  conduit and VID, so the firmware got one rule per port and sent one
  copy per port, each of which the switch delivered to all the members.
  Keep one rule per distinct output. ds2bkp found this and the
  membership problem first (openwrt-xiaomi-ax3000t-rd03v2, their ECM
  0030); this is the same idea on the VLAN hierarchy of 0051.
- The egress VLAN tags were reset for the slot of the vif being looked
  at, not for the rule being filled in, which is a different one once a
  vif has been skipped: a skipped vif's tag stayed in the next rule.
- bridge_update_connections() took a second reference to the front end
  on the update path and dropped one, and none when nothing changed.

Signed-off-by: Stanislaw Pal <kuncy7@gmail.com>
---
diff --git a/ecm_interface.c b/ecm_interface.c
index c8c3b9a..aab4a68 100644
--- a/ecm_interface.c
+++ b/ecm_interface.c
@@ -1097,6 +1097,38 @@ static inline bool ecm_interface_is_vlan_dev(struct net_device *dev)
 	       (ecm_interface_dsa_8021q_conduit(dev, NULL) != NULL && !ecm_interface_dsa_8021q_vlan_aware(dev, NULL));
 }
 
+static int ecm_interface_dsa_8021q_lower(struct net_device *lower, struct netdev_nested_priv *priv)
+{
+	return ecm_interface_dsa_8021q_conduit(lower, NULL) != NULL;
+}
+
+/*
+ * ecm_interface_bridge_has_dsa_8021q_port()
+ *	Whether a port of the bridge is a DSA user port under a tag_8021q
+ *	tagger.
+ *
+ * The multicast rule of such a bridge names the conduit and the VID the
+ * tagger gives the bridge, the same for every port of it, and the switch
+ * delivers the frames to the ports itself; which ports want a group is the
+ * bridge's to say, not something the rule can be edited for port by port.
+ */
+bool ecm_interface_bridge_has_dsa_8021q_port(struct net_device *brdev)
+{
+	struct netdev_nested_priv priv = { };
+	bool found;
+
+	if (!brdev || !netif_is_bridge_master(brdev)) {
+		return false;
+	}
+
+	rcu_read_lock();
+	found = netdev_walk_all_lower_dev_rcu(brdev, ecm_interface_dsa_8021q_lower, &priv) != 0;
+	rcu_read_unlock();
+
+	return found;
+}
+EXPORT_SYMBOL(ecm_interface_bridge_has_dsa_8021q_port);
